<div class="section" id="s1">
  <h2 class="section-title">1. What is Web Application Security?</h2>

  <p>Imagine your web application as a bank branch. The building has a front door (the login page), teller windows (APIs), a vault (the database), and security cameras (logs). <strong>Web application security</strong> is the discipline of ensuring that every door, window, and camera in that branch is designed, configured, and monitored so that only the right people get access to the right things.</p>

  <p>Every time you visit a website, your browser sends <strong>HTTP requests</strong> to a server. Those requests carry data: usernames, passwords, search terms, form inputs. If the server doesn't carefully validate and sanitize that data, an attacker can slip in malicious payloads and make the server do things it was never supposed to do — delete records, steal credentials, expose private files, or even execute commands on the underlying operating system.</p>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Why Does It Matter?</h3>
  <p>Web applications are the most targeted attack surface in modern computing. Unlike a desktop program that only runs on your machine, a web app is accessible from anywhere on the internet — 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. Every exposed endpoint is a potential entry point for an attacker scanning the globe for vulnerabilities.</p>

  <div class="callout info">
    <div class="callout-icon">📊</div>
    <div class="callout-body">
      <strong>Industry Fact</strong>
      <p>According to Verizon's Data Breach Investigations Report, web application attacks account for over 40% of all breaches. The average cost of a data breach in 2024 crossed $4.8 million USD — making prevention far cheaper than remediation.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub-title">The CIA Triad</h3>
  <p>All security objectives revolve around three pillars:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Confidentiality</strong> — Sensitive data should only be readable by authorized parties. (Example: only you should be able to read your inbox.)</li>
    <li><strong>Integrity</strong> — Data should not be altered without authorization. (Example: your bank balance should only change when you make a legitimate transaction.)</li>
    <li><strong>Availability</strong> — Services should remain accessible to legitimate users. (Example: your banking app should work even if someone tries to flood it with fake traffic.)</li>
  </ul>

