<div class="section" id="s2">
  <h2 class="section-title">2. OWASP Top 10 — All Categories with Real-World Examples</h2>

  <p>The <strong>Open Web Application Security Project (OWASP)</strong> is a nonprofit foundation that maintains a globally recognized list of the ten most critical web application security risks. Think of it as the "FBI's Most Wanted" list for web vulnerabilities — reviewed by thousands of security professionals and updated every few years based on real breach data.</p>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <div class="callout-icon">📋</div>
    <div class="callout-body">
      <strong>Current Edition: OWASP Top 10 — 2021</strong>
      <p>The 2021 list introduced three new categories and reorganized several existing ones. It reflects data from over 500,000 applications and input from 40+ industry experts.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="owasp-grid">

    <div class="owasp-card" data-num="1">
      <div class="owasp-num">A01 · 2021</div>
      <div class="owasp-name">Broken Access Control</div>
      <div class="owasp-desc">Users can act outside their intended permissions — accessing other users' data, admin functions, or unauthorized resources. Moved to #1 from #5 in 2017, found in 94% of tested apps.</div>
      <div class="owasp-example"><strong>Real Example →</strong> In 2019, a Facebook bug allowed any user to delete anyone else's photos by manipulating an API parameter. The researcher earned a $10,000 bug bounty.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="owasp-card" data-num="2">
      <div class="owasp-num">A02 · 2021</div>
      <div class="owasp-name">Cryptographic Failures</div>
      <div class="owasp-desc">Sensitive data (passwords, credit cards, health records) is exposed due to weak encryption, missing encryption, or improper key management. Previously called "Sensitive Data Exposure."</div>
      <div class="owasp-example"><strong>Real Example →</strong> Adobe's 2013 breach exposed 153 million records with passwords encrypted using 3DES (a weak, reversible cipher) instead of proper hashing. Entire password lists were reconstructed.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="owasp-card" data-num="3">
      <div class="owasp-num">A03 · 2021</div>
      <div class="owasp-name">Injection</div>
      <div class="owasp-desc">Untrusted data is sent to an interpreter as part of a command or query. SQL, NoSQL, OS command, LDAP, and XSS injections all fall here. An attacker tricks the server into executing unintended instructions.</div>
      <div class="owasp-example"><strong>Real Example →</strong> The 2008 Heartland Payment Systems breach used SQL Injection to steal 130 million credit card numbers — the largest card breach in history at the time.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="owasp-card" data-num="4">
      <div class="owasp-num">A04 · 2021</div>
      <div class="owasp-name">Insecure Design</div>
      <div class="owasp-desc">Flaws in how the application is architectured — missing threat modeling, absent security controls in design. A new category in 2021, it captures risks that can't be fixed by just patching code.</div>
      <div class="owasp-example"><strong>Real Example →</strong> An airline's "forgot password" flow sent a reset link to an email visible in a JavaScript variable on the page — a design flaw that allowed account takeover without any server-side exploit.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="owasp-card" data-num="5">
      <div class="owasp-num">A05 · 2021</div>
      <div class="owasp-name">Security Misconfiguration</div>
      <div class="owasp-desc">Default credentials, verbose error messages, open cloud storage buckets, unnecessary ports open, missing security headers. The most commonly found issue in practice.</div>
      <div class="owasp-example"><strong>Real Example →</strong> In 2019, Capital One suffered a breach exposing 100 million customers due to a misconfigured AWS Web Application Firewall that allowed SSRF attacks against EC2 metadata.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="owasp-card" data-num="6">
      <div class="owasp-num">A06 · 2021</div>
      <div class="owasp-name">Vulnerable &amp; Outdated Components</div>
      <div class="owasp-desc">Libraries, frameworks, and other software components that have known vulnerabilities and are not updated. The application is only as secure as its weakest dependency.</div>
      <div class="owasp-example"><strong>Real Example →</strong> The Equifax breach (2017) exposed 147 million records because the company hadn't patched Apache Struts 2 — two months after a critical patch was publicly released.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="owasp-card" data-num="7">
      <div class="owasp-num">A07 · 2021</div>
      <div class="owasp-name">Identification &amp; Authentication Failures</div>
      <div class="owasp-desc">Weak or incorrectly implemented authentication — no brute-force protection, plain-text passwords, broken session management, missing MFA. Previously "Broken Authentication."</div>
      <div class="owasp-example"><strong>Real Example →</strong> The 2012 LinkedIn breach exposed 117 million passwords hashed with SHA-1 and no salt — a weakness that allowed attackers to crack most passwords within days using rainbow tables.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="owasp-card" data-num="8">
      <div class="owasp-num">A08 · 2021</div>
      <div class="owasp-name">Software &amp; Data Integrity Failures</div>
      <div class="owasp-desc">Assumptions about software updates, CI/CD pipelines, and critical data made without verifying integrity. Includes insecure deserialization. New in 2021.</div>
      <div class="owasp-example"><strong>Real Example →</strong> The SolarWinds attack (2020) compromised the software update pipeline, embedding malware into a legitimate update delivered to 18,000+ organizations including US government agencies.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="owasp-card" data-num="9">
      <div class="owasp-num">A09 · 2021</div>
      <div class="owasp-name">Security Logging &amp; Monitoring Failures</div>
      <div class="owasp-desc">Insufficient logging, monitoring, and alerting means breaches go undetected. The average time to detect a breach is 207 days — logging failures are a key reason why.</div>
      <div class="owasp-example"><strong>Real Example →</strong> In the Yahoo breaches (2013–2014), attackers had access for over 3 years before discovery. Inadequate monitoring and logging made detection nearly impossible.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="owasp-card" data-num="10">
      <div class="owasp-num">A10 · 2021</div>
      <div class="owasp-name">Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)</div>
      <div class="owasp-desc">The server is tricked into making HTTP requests to unintended locations — internal services, cloud metadata APIs, or localhost resources not accessible from the internet.</div>
      <div class="owasp-example"><strong>Real Example →</strong> The Capital One breach involved SSRF against AWS metadata at 169.254.169.254, which returned IAM credentials that allowed the attacker to access S3 buckets containing customer data.</div>
    </div>

  </div>
</div>

