<div class="section" id="s6">
  <h2 class="section-title">6. SSRF — Server-Side Request Forgery</h2>

  <p><strong>Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)</strong> is a vulnerability where an attacker can make the server send HTTP requests to unintended destinations. The server acts as a proxy on the attacker's behalf — reaching internal systems, cloud metadata endpoints, or even other servers behind firewalls that the attacker could never access directly.</p>

  <h3 class="sub-title">The Analogy</h3>
  <p>Imagine you can't enter a secure government building, but you can send letters to a clerk inside. If that clerk blindly delivers any package you specify — even ones addressed to <em>internal departments</em> — they're being exploited as an SSRF middleman. You never needed a security badge; you just needed to trick the clerk.</p>

