  <h3 class="sub-title">Common SSRF Targets</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><code>http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/</code> — AWS EC2 instance metadata (IAM keys, network config)</li>
    <li><code>http://localhost:6379</code> — Redis cache (often unauthenticated on internal network)</li>
    <li><code>http://192.168.1.1</code> — Internal router admin panel</li>
    <li><code>file:///etc/passwd</code> — Local file inclusion via file:// scheme</li>
    <li>Internal microservices not exposed to the internet</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="sub-title">SSRF Bypass Techniques</h3>
  <div class="code-block">
    <div class="code-label">Common Bypasses</div>
    <pre><span class="c-cm">// IP address obfuscation:</span>
http://<span class="c-num">2130706433</span>/          <span class="c-cm">← decimal for 127.0.0.1</span>
http://<span class="c-num">0x7f000001</span>/          <span class="c-cm">← hex for 127.0.0.1</span>
http://<span class="c-str">127.0.0.1</span>.<span class="c-str">nip.io</span>/    <span class="c-cm">← DNS resolves to 127.0.0.1</span>

<span class="c-cm">// Protocol tricks:</span>
dict://localhost:<span class="c-num">6379</span>/info
gopher://localhost:<span class="c-num">25</span>/_EHLO%20attacker.com</pre>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Prevention</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Validate and allowlist URLs — only permit known, necessary external domains</li>
    <li>Block requests to private IP ranges (RFC 1918) and loopback addresses on the server side</li>
    <li>Disable unnecessary URL-fetching features</li>
    <li>Use a dedicated egress proxy with strict filtering</li>
    <li>Disable cloud metadata access if not needed, or use IMDSv2 (token-based metadata)</li>
  </ul>
</div>

