<!-- SECTION 7 — BROKEN AUTH -->
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="s7">
  <h2 class="section-title">7. Broken Authentication</h2>

  <p><strong>Broken Authentication</strong> covers flaws in how applications verify who you are and how they manage your session after you log in. Even if your SQL and XSS defenses are perfect, weak authentication means any attacker who guesses or steals credentials walks right through the front door.</p>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Common Authentication Weaknesses</h3>

  <table class="compare-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Weakness</th><th>What It Means</th><th>Impact</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>No brute-force protection</td><td>Unlimited login attempts allowed</td><td>Password guessing, credential stuffing</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Weak default credentials</td><td><code>admin/admin</code>, <code>admin/password</code></td><td>Immediate admin access</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Plaintext passwords</td><td>Stored without hashing</td><td>Full exposure in a DB breach</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Weak hashing (MD5/SHA1)</td><td>Fast to crack with GPU rainbow tables</td><td>Passwords cracked in seconds</td></tr>
      <tr><td>No MFA</td><td>Single factor authentication only</td><td>Stolen password = full account access</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Predictable tokens</td><td>Session IDs based on timestamp or username</td><td>Session hijacking</td></tr>
      <tr><td>No session expiry</td><td>Sessions never time out</td><td>Permanent access from stolen cookie</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Credential Stuffing</h3>
  <p>When a large site like LinkedIn or RockYou gets breached, hundreds of millions of email/password pairs are dumped online. Attackers then <em>stuff</em> these combinations into other sites automatically — because most people reuse passwords. This is why even a well-secured application can be compromised by a breach at a completely different service.</p>

  <div class="callout danger">
    <div class="callout-icon">🚨</div>
    <div class="callout-body">
      <strong>Real Case: RockYou2024</strong>
      <p>In 2024, a massive compilation dataset called RockYou2024 was posted containing nearly 10 billion unique passwords — assembled from decades of data breaches. Credential stuffing tools can test these against any login form automatically.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Session Management Flaws</h3>
  <div class="code-block">
    <div class="code-label">Session Security Checklist</div>
    <pre><span class="c-cm">✅ Generate session ID server-side using cryptographically secure random bytes</span>
<span class="c-cm">✅ Set cookie flags: HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Strict</span>
<span class="c-cm">✅ Regenerate session ID after login (prevents session fixation)</span>
<span class="c-cm">✅ Invalidate session on logout (server-side deletion)</span>
<span class="c-cm">✅ Set appropriate expiry (absolute + idle timeout)</span>

<span class="c-cm">❌ Never store session IDs in URLs (appear in logs, referrer headers)</span>
<span class="c-cm">❌ Never use predictable values like MD5(username + timestamp)</span></pre>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Secure Password Storage: bcrypt</h3>
  <div class="code-block">
    <div class="code-label">Python — Secure Password Hashing</div>
    <pre><span class="c-kw">import</span> bcrypt

<span class="c-cm"># Hashing a password (cost factor 12 = ~250ms per hash)</span>
<span class="c-var">password</span> = <span class="c-str">b"MySecretPass123"</span>
<span class="c-var">hashed</span> = bcrypt.<span class="c-fn">hashpw</span>(<span class="c-var">password</span>, bcrypt.<span class="c-fn">gensalt</span>(rounds=<span class="c-num">12</span>))

<span class="c-cm"># Verifying (never compare raw passwords!)</span>
<span class="c-kw">if</span> bcrypt.<span class="c-fn">checkpw</span>(<span class="c-var">entered_password</span>, <span class="c-var">hashed</span>):
    <span class="c-fn">grant_access</span>()

<span class="c-cm"># Why bcrypt? Each hash takes 250ms → 250ms per guess
# At 4 guesses/second, 10⁸ common passwords = 800 years</span></pre>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- SECTION 8 — BURP SUITE -->
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="s8">
  <h2 class="section-title">8. Burp Suite — Web Interception</h2>

  <p><strong>Burp Suite</strong> by PortSwigger is the industry-standard toolkit for web application security testing. It sits between your browser and the target server as a proxy, allowing you to inspect, modify, replay, and fuzz every HTTP request and response. Think of it as an X-ray machine for web traffic.</p>

  <h3 class="sub-title">How Burp Suite Works</h3>

  <div class="flow-diagram">
    <div class="flow-node"><span class="fn-icon">🌐</span><span class="fn-label">Your Browser</span></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
    <div class="flow-node" style="border-color:var(--accent);"><span class="fn-icon">🔍</span><span class="fn-label">Burp Proxy<br><span style="font-size:0.65rem;color:var(--accent)">127.0.0.1:8080</span></span></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
    <div class="flow-node"><span class="fn-icon">🖥️</span><span class="fn-label">Target Server</span></div>
  </div>

  <p>You configure your browser to send traffic through Burp's proxy (running on <code>127.0.0.1:8080</code>). Every request is caught, displayed, and held — you can read it, modify it, then choose to forward it. Burp also intercepts responses from the server.</p>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Key Burp Suite Tools</h3>

  <h4 class="mini-title">1. Proxy — Intercept &amp; Modify Requests</h4>
  <p>The core feature. Enable Intercept mode and every HTTP request pauses for your inspection. Change form values, headers, cookies, HTTP methods — then forward or drop. Ideal for testing authentication bypass and parameter manipulation.</p>

  <h4 class="mini-title">2. Repeater — Replay &amp; Tweak Requests</h4>
  <p>Send a request to Repeater and fire it as many times as you like with modifications. Essential for manually exploiting SQLi, XSS, and IDOR — you can see exactly what response each payload generates, side by side.</p>

  <h4 class="mini-title">3. Intruder — Automated Fuzzing</h4>
  <p>Select a parameter, define a payload list, and let Intruder systematically send thousands of variations. Common uses:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Brute-forcing login credentials with a wordlist</li>
    <li>Fuzzing IDOR parameters (try IDs 1 through 10,000)</li>
    <li>Finding hidden directories or parameters</li>
  </ul>

  <h4 class="mini-title">4. Scanner (Pro) — Automated Vulnerability Detection</h4>
  <p>Burp Suite Professional's active scanner crawls the application and automatically tests for hundreds of vulnerabilities — including SQLi, XSS, SSRF, XXE, and more. Community Edition has a passive scanner only.</p>

  <h4 class="mini-title">5. Decoder — Encode/Decode Data</h4>
  <p>Convert data between URL encoding, Base64, HTML entities, hex, binary, and more. Essential for understanding obfuscated payloads and crafting your own.</p>

  <h4 class="mini-title">6. Comparer — Diff Responses</h4>
  <p>Compare two responses side by side — pixel-perfect diffing to spot subtle differences caused by your payloads. Critical for Blind SQLi where you're looking for tiny behavioral changes.</p>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Step-by-Step: Setting Up Burp Suite</h3>
  <ol class="step-list">
    <li>Download and install Burp Suite Community Edition from PortSwigger's website (Java-based, runs on Windows/Linux/Mac).</li>
    <li>Start Burp and create a Temporary Project. Go to <strong>Proxy → Options</strong> and note the listener: <code>127.0.0.1:8080</code>.</li>
    <li>Configure your browser proxy to <code>127.0.0.1:8080</code>. In Firefox: Settings → Network Settings → Manual Proxy Configuration.</li>
    <li>Install Burp's CA certificate: browse to <code>http://burpsuite</code> in your browser and download the certificate. Add it to your browser's trusted authorities to avoid SSL errors on HTTPS sites.</li>
    <li>Enable Intercept under <strong>Proxy → Intercept</strong>. Browse to your target and watch requests appear in Burp's interface.</li>
    <li>Right-click any request to send it to Repeater, Intruder, or Scanner for further testing.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout success">
    <div class="callout-icon">💡</div>
    <div class="callout-body">
      <strong>Pro Tip: Burp's Target Scope</strong>
      <p>Set a Target Scope (in <strong>Target → Scope</strong>) to your test domain only. This prevents Burp from logging irrelevant browser traffic (Google, Facebook, etc.) and keeps your testing focused and clean.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Reading a Raw HTTP Request in Burp</h3>
