<!-- SECTION 9 — DVWA -->
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="s9">
  <h2 class="section-title">9. DVWA — Practice Lab</h2>

  <p><strong>Damn Vulnerable Web Application (DVWA)</strong> is a deliberately insecure PHP/MySQL web application designed for security professionals to practice their skills in a legal, controlled environment. It includes exercises at three difficulty levels — Low, Medium, and High — for each vulnerability type.</p>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <div class="callout-icon">⚠️</div>
    <div class="callout-body">
      <strong>Legal Reminder</strong>
      <p>DVWA must only be run on your own machine or VM, on an isolated network. Never expose it to the internet. Attacking systems without explicit permission is illegal and unethical, regardless of the vulnerability.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Setting Up DVWA in Your VM</h3>
  <ol class="step-list">
    <li>Download and install <strong>VirtualBox</strong> or VMware Workstation on your host machine.</li>
    <li>Download <strong>Kali Linux</strong> (or Ubuntu) as your VM image — Kali comes pre-loaded with security tools including Burp Suite.</li>
    <li>Install DVWA: <code>sudo apt install dvwa</code> on Kali, or clone from GitHub and configure with Apache/MySQL.</li>
    <li>Edit DVWA's config: set database credentials, set <code>$_DVWA['default_security_level'] = 'low'</code> to start.</li>
    <li>Visit <code>http://127.0.0.1/dvwa</code> in your browser. Login: <code>admin / password</code>. Click <em>Create/Reset Database</em>.</li>
    <li>Set Burp Suite as your browser proxy and start practicing — begin with SQL Injection at Low security.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3 class="sub-title">DVWA Module Walkthrough Guide</h3>

  <table class="compare-table">
    <thead><tr><th>DVWA Module</th><th>What to Practice</th><th>Key Payload to Try</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>SQL Injection</td><td>Classic, Blind, Union-based</td><td><code>' OR 1=1-- </code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>SQL Injection (Blind)</td><td>Boolean and time-based extraction</td><td><code>' AND SLEEP(5)-- </code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>XSS (Reflected)</td><td>Payload in URL parameter</td><td><code>&lt;script&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt;</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>XSS (Stored)</td><td>Stored script in guestbook</td><td><code>&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(document.cookie)&gt;</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>XSS (DOM)</td><td>DOM manipulation via URL fragment</td><td><code>#&lt;script&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt;</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>CSRF</td><td>Forge password change requests</td><td>Craft a malicious HTML form</td></tr>
      <tr><td>File Inclusion</td><td>LFI and RFI exploitation</td><td><code>?page=../../etc/passwd</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>File Upload</td><td>Upload PHP webshell</td><td>Upload <code>shell.php</code> disguised as image</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Command Injection</td><td>OS command execution via form</td><td><code>127.0.0.1; ls -la</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Brute Force</td><td>Login brute force with Intruder</td><td>Use <code>rockyou.txt</code> wordlist</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Progression Strategy</h3>
  <p>The ideal DVWA learning path:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Complete each module at <strong>Low</strong> security — understand the vulnerability, read the source code, understand why it works.</li>
    <li>Move to <strong>Medium</strong> — the application adds basic defenses. Try to bypass them. Understand why basic sanitization isn't enough.</li>
    <li>Try <strong>High</strong> — some modules are effectively secure at this level. Understand what makes the defense solid.</li>
    <li>Read the PHP source for each level using the "View Source" button — source code review is a critical real-world skill.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

