<!-- SECTION 10 — PENTEST REPORTS -->
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="s10">
  <h2 class="section-title">10. Writing Basic Vulnerability Descriptions for a Pentest Report</h2>

  <p>A penetration test is only as valuable as the report it produces. Even the most devastating finding is useless if the development team can't understand it or if management can't assess the business risk. A good vulnerability description communicates clearly to both technical and non-technical audiences.</p>

  <h3 class="sub-title">The Anatomy of a Vulnerability Finding</h3>
  <p>Every finding in a professional pentest report should contain these fields:</p>

  <div class="report-box">
    <div class="report-field"><label>Title</label><span>SQL Injection in Login Form</span></div>
    <div class="report-field"><label>Severity</label><span><span class="severity-badge sev-critical">Critical</span> &nbsp; CVSS Score: 9.8 / 10.0</span></div>
    <div class="report-field"><label>Affected URL</label><span><code>https://target.com/login</code> — POST parameter: <code>username</code></span></div>
    <div class="report-field"><label>Description</label><span>The application fails to sanitize user-supplied input in the <code>username</code> field before incorporating it into a SQL query. An unauthenticated attacker can manipulate the query to bypass authentication, extract all user credentials, or modify/delete database records.</span></div>
    <div class="report-field"><label>Evidence</label><span>Submitting <code>' OR '1'='1'--</code> as the username resulted in a successful login without a valid password. See Appendix A for Burp Suite screenshots.</span></div>
    <div class="report-field"><label>Business Impact</label><span>An attacker could gain administrative access to the application, expose all customer personally identifiable information (PII), and potentially achieve remote code execution, resulting in full server compromise and regulatory liability under GDPR/IT Act 2000.</span></div>
    <div class="report-field"><label>Remediation</label><span>Replace string concatenation in the login query with parameterized queries (prepared statements). Implement input validation to reject unexpected characters. Apply principle of least privilege to the database account used by the application.</span></div>
    <div class="report-field"><label>References</label><span>OWASP A03:2021 — Injection; CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements</span></div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Severity Rating System</h3>
  <p>Use the <strong>CVSS (Common Vulnerability Scoring System)</strong> or a simplified framework to communicate risk consistently:</p>

  <table class="compare-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Severity</th><th>CVSS Range</th><th>Typical Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="severity-badge sev-critical">Critical</span></td><td>9.0 – 10.0</td><td>Immediate, severe impact. Unauthenticated RCE or mass data exposure.</td><td>Unauthenticated SQLi → full DB dump</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="severity-badge sev-high">High</span></td><td>7.0 – 8.9</td><td>Significant impact requiring authentication or some user interaction.</td><td>Stored XSS → account takeover</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="severity-badge sev-medium">Medium</span></td><td>4.0 – 6.9</td><td>Moderate impact. Limited data exposure or requires complex attack chain.</td><td>IDOR exposing non-sensitive data</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag">Low</span></td><td>0.1 – 3.9</td><td>Minor impact. Informational, limited exploitability.</td><td>Verbose error message (stack trace)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Sample Findings for Common Vulnerabilities</h3>

  <div class="report-box">
    <div class="report-field"><label>Title</label><span>Reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)</span></div>
    <div class="report-field"><label>Severity</label><span><span class="severity-badge sev-high">High</span></span></div>
    <div class="report-field"><label>Affected URL</label><span><code>https://target.com/search?q=[payload]</code></span></div>
    <div class="report-field"><label>Description</label><span>User-supplied input in the <code>q</code> parameter is reflected in the HTTP response without proper HTML encoding. An attacker can craft a URL containing JavaScript that executes in a victim's browser when they click the link, allowing session hijacking, phishing, or malicious actions performed on the victim's behalf.</span></div>
    <div class="report-field"><label>Evidence</label><span>Payload <code>&lt;script&gt;alert(document.domain)&lt;/script&gt;</code> caused a JavaScript alert dialog to appear, confirming script execution in the context of the application domain.</span></div>
    <div class="report-field"><label>Remediation</label><span>Encode all user-supplied data before rendering in HTML responses. Implement a Content Security Policy (CSP) header. Validate and reject input containing HTML special characters where plain text is expected.</span></div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub-title">Report Writing Tips</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Be specific</strong> — Always include the exact URL, parameter, method, and payload that demonstrated the vulnerability.</li>
    <li><strong>Use screenshots</strong> — Burp Suite screenshots showing the raw request and the proof-of-concept response are essential evidence.</li>
    <li><strong>Separate technical and executive sections</strong> — Executives need business impact; developers need technical detail and remediation code.</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid jargon without explanation</strong> — If you write "IDOR," follow it with a one-sentence plain-English definition.</li>
    <li><strong>Prioritize clearly</strong> — Use a severity system and help the team understand which findings to fix first.</li>
    <li><strong>Include false positives</strong> — Note what you tested but did not find vulnerable — it shows thoroughness.</li>
    <li><strong>Be constructive</strong> — Your goal is to help the team fix things, not to sound impressive. Clear, actionable remediation beats vague advice every time.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

